Record the human's approval of the brief, bound to its text; refused while `brief check` has blockers

Usage: canon brief approve [OPTIONS] --approver <NAME> --note <TEXT> [CHANGE_ID]...

Arguments:
  [CHANGE_ID]...  The changes to approve, one answer for all, none written unless every one is approvable (default: the repo's only live change)

Options:
      --approver <NAME>      Who approved (the human, not the agent)
      --as <ROLE>            The role you act as: planner, implementer, reviewer or orchestrator. Overrides `CANON_ROLE`. `schema` and `context` list only the role's commands, `status` keeps its steps, and `--role` on `evidence add` / `evidence audit` / `review scenario` / `review change` defaults to it [possible values: planner, implementer, reviewer, orchestrator]
      --note <TEXT>          The human's own words
      --repo <DIR>           The repo to act on (default: the nearest ancestor directory holding a canon.yaml)
      --json                 Output JSON instead of the human-readable form
      --input <JSON|FILE|->  The arguments as JSON: an object (one run) or an array / NDJSON file or `-` (a batch); keys are the option names in camelCase, `operands` an array
  -h, --help                 Print help
  -V, --version              Print version

Examples:
  canon brief approve add-login --approver Ana --note 'Go. Ship the failure flow too.'
  canon brief approve add-login add-search fix-sync --approver Ana --note 'All three, go.'

Several ids take one name, one answer and one timestamp. Every id is checked
first; one refused id refuses the call (BRIEF_APPROVE_REFUSED) and nothing is
written. Writes `Approved: <who> <when> — "<words>" (brief sha256:<digest>)` into the
brief (the digest covers the brief without its `Approved:` lines) and records
`canon policy approve` in the same call. Edit the brief afterwards and it reads as
not approved (BRIEF_CHANGED_SINCE_APPROVAL) until a human answers again and this
runs again. Run it only from the human's answer, with their words as --note.
